# `stands as`, `serves as`, and `acts as` dress up `is` as a role (`the
# index serves as a lookup table` becomes `the index is a lookup table`,
# and `the driver acts as a proxy` becomes `the driver proxies`). Each
# needs a determiner (a word that introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`,
# or `this`) after `as`, because the other readings compare or qualify
# (`stands as tall as`, `stands as written`, `serves as many requests
# as`, `serves as root`, `serves as-is`).
expr main [(stands as DET), (serves as DET), ([acts, act] as DET)]
let message "`serves as`, `acts as`, or `stands as`. Write `is`, or the verb the role names."
let description "Flags `stands as`, `serves as`, and `acts as` before a noun phrase, where `is` carries the meaning."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The cache stands in front of the database."
allows "The tower stands as tall as the building beside it."
allows "The regulation stands as written until the appeal is decided."
allows "The record stands as of this morning."
allows "The server serves as many requests as the socket buffer allows."
allows "The daemon serves as root, which is the reason for the seccomp filter."
allows "The device serves as-is with no warranty."
allows "The daemon serves as it always has, on port 80."
allows "The daemon acts as root while it binds the port."
